WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.920
You know, usually when we talk about the hard sciences, classical physics in particular,

00:05.780 --> 00:11.000
there's this expectation of just absolute uncompromising precision.

00:11.380 --> 00:13.680
Oh, absolutely. We completely rely on it.

00:13.760 --> 00:18.540
Right. I mean, if you want to calculate the exact trajectory of a billiard ball or, you know,

00:18.580 --> 00:24.000
the orbital mechanics you need to land a rover on Mars, you just write down the equations.

00:24.140 --> 00:25.980
You plug in the mass, the velocity.

00:25.980 --> 00:32.800
Exactly. The gravitational constants. And the universe just behaves exactly as the math dictates. It's clean.

00:33.060 --> 00:34.280
It's very clean.

00:34.420 --> 00:42.100
We like things to be perfectly categorized and reduced to their fundamental localized interactions.

00:42.280 --> 00:49.560
And, I mean, that reductionism has basically been the ultimate engine of scientific progress for, what, over three centuries?

00:49.880 --> 00:50.680
Yeah, easily.

00:50.680 --> 00:59.000
We break complex phenomena down into smaller and smaller discrete components like particles, fields, forces.

00:59.420 --> 00:59.540
Right.

00:59.800 --> 01:07.460
The whole assumption is that if we can just build a comprehensive catalog of all the atomic and subatomic microstates,

01:07.680 --> 01:14.000
then the macro level behavior of the universe, it just sort of emerges as a computational inevitability.

01:14.000 --> 01:18.820
But then you step into the world of biological life, right?

01:19.160 --> 01:22.700
A living, breathing, adapting organism.

01:23.380 --> 01:29.840
And suddenly that pristine mathematical machinery just seems to, well, grind its gears.

01:29.980 --> 01:34.340
It really does. The purely physico-chemical equations just hit a brick wall.

01:34.600 --> 01:41.980
Yeah. And for a long time, the standard excuse has basically been that biology is simply a computational scaling problem.

01:41.980 --> 01:44.480
Right. The too many variables argument.

01:44.620 --> 01:50.800
Exactly. People say, oh, there are too many variables, too many molecules bouncing around inside a single eukaryotic cell.

01:51.060 --> 01:51.360
Yeah.

01:51.760 --> 01:59.520
And the argument goes, you know, if we just had a quantum computer powerful enough to track every single atomic interaction,

02:00.360 --> 02:08.600
then classical and quantum physics could explain a human being just as easily as they explain the orbit of Jupiter.

02:08.600 --> 02:13.500
Which is a deeply, deeply entrenched assumption in the scientific community.

02:13.640 --> 02:14.220
I told you.

02:14.340 --> 02:18.580
But our source material today completely inverts that entire premise.

02:18.980 --> 02:21.200
Yeah. And I am so excited to get into this.

02:21.320 --> 02:26.560
We are looking at a really paradigm-shifting paper today by the independent researcher Affliction.

02:27.180 --> 02:34.760
It's titled Closure of Closures, Life, Sheaves, and the Re-Glowing of Fractured Autonomy.

02:34.760 --> 02:36.080
It's quite a title.

02:36.420 --> 02:47.200
It is. And it builds on the critique of physico-chemical reductionism that was articulated in the book The Blind Spot by Frank Gleiser and Thompson.

02:47.440 --> 02:47.480
Right.

02:47.840 --> 02:57.440
And what Affliction proposes here is that the failure of physics to fully explain biological life is not a computational deficit.

02:57.740 --> 03:01.360
Like, it's not about biology just being too complex.

03:01.360 --> 03:09.440
No, it's not a lack of computing power. It's actually a fundamental structural flaw in the mathematical language of physics itself.

03:09.540 --> 03:19.520
Okay, let's unpack this. Because the premise here is that the ontology of standard physics is literally blind to the structure of life.

03:19.660 --> 03:20.020
Yes.

03:20.300 --> 03:25.380
The equations don't just, you know, struggle to compute life. They systematically erase it.

03:25.460 --> 03:27.140
They wipe it right off the map.

03:27.140 --> 03:36.140
So today we're going to explore how affliction translates this deeply philosophical mystery into a rigorous, computable mathematical theorem.

03:36.980 --> 03:46.260
And we're going to look at how redefining life as a topological loop fundamentally restructures our entire understanding of autopoiesis.

03:46.600 --> 03:55.480
Right. And how scaling that math up could completely revolutionize the future of pathology and medicine, which is just wild to think about.

03:55.480 --> 04:06.060
It really is. But, I mean, to understand why physics possesses this inherent blind spot in the first place, we have to look at what the paper calls the projection error.

04:06.440 --> 04:07.700
Okay, yeah, let's set it there.

04:07.840 --> 04:13.960
So this is a concept formalized in the paper based on a really fascinating thought experiment from the blind spot.

04:14.100 --> 04:14.400
Right.

04:14.900 --> 04:22.600
Imagine, if you will, an artificial intelligence that is equipped with perfect, exhaustive knowledge of physics and chemistry.

04:22.820 --> 04:24.940
Okay, so like a super physicist AI.

04:24.940 --> 04:37.280
Exactly. This AI has no human biases, no biological instincts at all, just pure, unadulterated access to the fundamental laws of nature.

04:37.540 --> 04:37.960
Got it.

04:38.140 --> 04:42.420
It can flawlessly calculate Navier-Stokes equations for fluid dynamics.

04:42.940 --> 04:48.060
It understands every thermodynamic gradient, every single molecular interaction.

04:48.060 --> 04:53.600
So we point this omniscient physicist AI at a human heart, right?

04:53.600 --> 04:53.720
Yeah.

04:53.720 --> 04:58.140
Just a heart beating in a chest, and we ask it to analyze what it sees.

04:58.360 --> 04:58.700
Right.

04:58.920 --> 05:04.560
And the AI observes the heart, and it basically registers two primary physical outputs.

05:05.360 --> 05:11.020
First, it observes volumetric displacement driven by shifting electrical gradients.

05:11.020 --> 05:13.260
Which is just blood pumping through the chambers.

05:13.740 --> 05:14.100
Exactly.

05:14.620 --> 05:19.260
And second, it observes acoustic waves propagating through a medium.

05:19.360 --> 05:20.900
The thump, thump sound.

05:21.020 --> 05:21.200
Right.

05:21.280 --> 05:24.400
The sound vibrating the surrounding tissue and the air.

05:24.580 --> 05:31.980
Now, from a purely physico-chemical perspective, those two outputs are entirely equal.

05:32.180 --> 05:33.500
They're just physical events.

05:33.700 --> 05:34.120
Exactly.

05:34.120 --> 05:38.240
They are simply transfers of mass and energy over time.

05:39.020 --> 05:45.320
The AI can model the fluid dynamics of the blood with absolute perfection, and it can model

05:45.320 --> 05:47.740
the acoustic waves with equal precision.

05:48.200 --> 05:48.420
Yeah.

05:48.540 --> 05:55.200
But nothing in that exhaustive state evolution description tells the AI that pumping blood

05:55.200 --> 05:56.480
is the heart's function.

05:56.680 --> 05:56.940
Right.

05:57.220 --> 06:02.980
While generating acoustic waves is merely an epiphenomenon, like just a side effect.

06:02.980 --> 06:08.500
Because standard physics inherently lacks the conceptual vocabulary for purpose or function.

06:08.760 --> 06:09.200
Exactly.

06:09.480 --> 06:12.060
Physics just tracks local cause and effect.

06:12.240 --> 06:14.260
A particle hits another particle.

06:14.740 --> 06:15.940
Energy converts to heat.

06:16.020 --> 06:16.220
Yeah.

06:16.340 --> 06:22.640
There's no operator in a Hamiltonian or Lagrangian mechanic that says,

06:23.360 --> 06:27.720
hey, this specific transfer energy is what this collection of matter is supposed to do.

06:28.120 --> 06:28.400
Right.

06:28.500 --> 06:28.920
Exactly.

06:28.920 --> 06:36.380
To select one physical effect as functional and just, you know, relegate another as incidental,

06:36.940 --> 06:40.520
you need an entirely different conceptual primitive.

06:40.900 --> 06:43.640
And that missing primitive is normativity.

06:43.960 --> 06:44.500
Normativity.

06:44.760 --> 06:45.000
Yeah.

06:45.640 --> 06:52.920
Philosophers of biology like Hans Jonas and Georges Campbellham, they've long argued that

06:52.920 --> 06:59.900
life introduces a built-in distinction between success and failure that inanimate matter just

06:59.900 --> 07:01.540
fundamentally lacks.

07:01.740 --> 07:07.220
And normativity in this biological sense, we should clarify, it's not about human moral

07:07.220 --> 07:07.580
value.

07:07.660 --> 07:07.960
No.

07:08.160 --> 07:08.820
No, not at all.

07:08.920 --> 07:10.220
It's a structural orientation.

07:10.680 --> 07:10.860
Yeah.

07:10.860 --> 07:17.280
A living system introduces a baseline of viability because it actively works to persist.

07:17.280 --> 07:17.840
Exactly.

07:18.180 --> 07:20.460
I mean, a rock doesn't care if it gets smashed into ground.

07:20.500 --> 07:20.720
Right.

07:20.860 --> 07:21.900
It's just smaller rocks.

07:22.000 --> 07:22.440
Exactly.

07:22.960 --> 07:27.460
The laws of physics describe the shattering perfectly, but the rock has no vested interest

07:27.460 --> 07:29.040
in remaining a solid rock.

07:29.260 --> 07:34.260
But a biological organism, on the other hand, it actively works against the thermodynamic

07:34.260 --> 07:37.720
gradients to maintain its own structural integrity.

07:37.940 --> 07:41.300
It has a physical orientation towards self-preservation.

07:41.300 --> 07:42.020
Right.

07:42.480 --> 07:48.200
Which means there's a mathematically definable difference between a viable state and a pathological

07:48.200 --> 07:48.580
state.

07:48.680 --> 07:49.100
Exactly.

07:49.560 --> 07:55.440
The problem is that standard physical equations map out trajectories in a phase space.

07:55.960 --> 07:59.780
Like state A transitions to state B, state B to state C.

07:59.940 --> 08:00.760
It's very linear.

08:00.980 --> 08:01.740
Very flat.

08:01.840 --> 08:07.280
So when you force a biologically normative system into that flat state evolution representation,

08:07.280 --> 08:12.400
you commit what fliction formalizes as the projection error.

08:12.820 --> 08:15.140
Let me try to visualize this for a second.

08:15.340 --> 08:21.780
So imagine a massive, complex roller coaster with giant loop-de-loops and corkscrews.

08:21.800 --> 08:21.980
Okay.

08:22.120 --> 08:22.700
I'm with you.

08:22.920 --> 08:27.820
Now imagine a light source shining straight down on it from above, casting a two-dimensional

08:27.820 --> 08:29.840
shadow on the flat ground below.

08:29.940 --> 08:30.260
Right.

08:30.600 --> 08:35.840
If you are a physicist who only studies the shadow, you see points moving, you see lines

08:35.840 --> 08:39.900
crossing, you can calculate the exact velocity of the shadow cars.

08:40.000 --> 08:40.260
Sure.

08:40.520 --> 08:42.840
But you completely missed the vertical dimension.

08:43.800 --> 08:46.100
The shadow entirely erases the loop.

08:46.260 --> 08:48.140
Yes, that's a brilliant analogy.

08:48.700 --> 08:51.500
You're looking at perfectly accurate local data.

08:51.660 --> 08:57.360
Like the shadow is a true projection of the coaster's coordinates, but you've fundamentally

08:57.360 --> 09:01.240
missed the topological reality of the loop itself.

09:01.420 --> 09:03.900
You don't get the thrill of the ride from the shadow.

09:03.900 --> 09:11.720
And that is exactly the essence of Proposition 1.1 in the paper, the non-invariance of closure

09:11.720 --> 09:12.920
under projection.

09:13.320 --> 09:13.520
Okay.

09:14.160 --> 09:19.440
Fliction actually provides a mathematical proof demonstrating that if you take a directed

09:19.440 --> 09:23.900
graph of biological processes that form a non-trivial cycle...

09:24.580 --> 09:24.820
A loop.

09:25.120 --> 09:25.840
Right, a loop.

09:25.840 --> 09:33.380
And you project it onto a standard state evolution model, the global topological property of that

09:33.380 --> 09:35.360
cycle is completely erased.

09:35.580 --> 09:35.900
Wow.

09:36.220 --> 09:41.500
Two entirely different graphs, one that loops back on itself to regenerate its starting

09:41.500 --> 09:46.740
conditions, and one that is just a linear sequence of reactions, they can produce the

09:46.740 --> 09:50.260
exact same localized state transitions in the shadow.

09:50.260 --> 09:52.540
Well, wait, let me push back on this for a second.

09:52.720 --> 09:52.840
Yeah.

09:52.900 --> 09:56.820
Isn't saying physics is wrong about life a bit of a stretch?

09:57.180 --> 10:04.240
I mean, we rely on physics for MRIs, for sequencing the genome, mapping the crystalline

10:04.240 --> 10:05.520
structures of proteins.

10:06.320 --> 10:10.180
Physics is doing an incredible amount of heavy lifting in modern biology.

10:10.280 --> 10:10.980
Oh, absolutely.

10:11.400 --> 10:14.480
And the paper makes a crucial distinction here.

10:15.140 --> 10:18.340
Physics is not making false predictions at the micro level.

10:18.340 --> 10:25.720
When an enzyme cleaves a substrate, the physical description of that localized cleavage is

10:25.720 --> 10:27.140
perfectly accurate.

10:27.300 --> 10:27.460
Right.

10:27.540 --> 10:29.360
The shadow is accurate to the shadow.

10:29.500 --> 10:29.960
Exactly.

10:30.540 --> 10:35.780
The issue is that the descriptive primitives of physics, you know, the particles, forces,

10:36.220 --> 10:42.840
fields, they just lack the topological operators necessary to capture a global cyclic network.

10:42.920 --> 10:43.420
I see.

10:43.740 --> 10:45.740
The 2D shadow isn't a lie.

10:45.740 --> 10:49.920
It simply lacks the dimensional capacity to encode the loop.

10:50.500 --> 10:56.560
When you project a system defined by closure into a flat state evolution framework, the mathematical

10:56.560 --> 10:59.180
signature of life literally disappears.

10:59.540 --> 11:06.220
So if the standard canvas of physics inherently erases the defining geometry of life, we basically

11:06.220 --> 11:08.080
need a new mathematical language.

11:08.240 --> 11:08.640
We do.

11:08.980 --> 11:11.800
We need a framework that natively preserves the loop.

11:11.800 --> 11:17.440
And that brings us out of the flatland of classical mechanics and into what the paper calls

11:17.440 --> 11:19.040
the RSVP field.

11:19.240 --> 11:19.580
Yes.

11:20.260 --> 11:26.860
The RSVP field is Flixian's translation of biological philosophy into a rigorous computable

11:26.860 --> 11:27.880
field theory.

11:28.500 --> 11:32.520
And RSVP stands for a few interconnected components, right?

11:33.280 --> 11:34.740
Let's break that acronym down.

11:34.860 --> 11:35.080
Right.

11:35.080 --> 11:41.160
So first we have a scalar density field, which is represented by the Greek letter phi.

11:41.480 --> 11:41.820
Okay.

11:42.120 --> 11:45.760
Then there is a vector transport field represented by V.

11:46.080 --> 11:46.520
Got it.

11:46.720 --> 11:50.360
And finally, an entropy density field represented by S.

11:50.540 --> 11:50.760
Okay.

11:50.860 --> 11:56.720
So let's break down how these variables actually interact to create a living region within this

11:56.720 --> 11:57.900
mathematical space.

11:58.020 --> 11:58.220
Sure.

11:58.220 --> 12:03.920
Because the paper formalizes this concept called constraint closure, and it draws heavily on

12:03.920 --> 12:09.760
complex systems theorists like Francisco Varela, Robert Rosen, and Stuart Kaufman.

12:10.080 --> 12:16.260
And constraint closure is really the absolute dividing line between a machine and an organism.

12:16.540 --> 12:17.080
How so?

12:17.420 --> 12:21.460
Well, consider a highly complex machine like a jet engine.

12:21.700 --> 12:22.020
Okay.

12:22.200 --> 12:25.220
The engine operates under extremely tight constraints.

12:25.220 --> 12:30.480
You've got the titanium compressor blades, the specific combustion chamber geometry.

12:31.040 --> 12:31.140
Right.

12:31.460 --> 12:32.680
Everything has to be perfect.

12:32.840 --> 12:33.280
Exactly.

12:33.760 --> 12:39.920
And these constraints force the explosive energy of the jet fuel to produce directed thrust,

12:40.160 --> 12:44.520
rather than just, you know, expanding randomly in all directions.

12:44.700 --> 12:50.840
But the jet engine does not forge its own titanium blades while flying at 30,000 feet.

12:51.080 --> 12:51.760
Precisely.

12:51.920 --> 12:54.500
The constraints are imposed from the outside.

12:54.500 --> 12:56.080
They are exogenous.

12:56.860 --> 12:56.880
Right.

12:57.060 --> 13:04.200
If a fan blade snaps, the engine cannot use the combustion of jet fuel to synthesize a replacement

13:04.200 --> 13:04.700
blade.

13:04.820 --> 13:05.560
It just crashes.

13:06.020 --> 13:06.260
Yeah.

13:06.460 --> 13:10.420
But an organism operates under fundamentally different topological rules.

13:10.540 --> 13:11.000
Exactly.

13:11.380 --> 13:14.720
A biological system also relies on constraints.

13:15.260 --> 13:22.440
You know, lipid bilayer membranes, intricately folded protein enzymes, regulatory DNA networks.

13:22.560 --> 13:22.800
Right.

13:22.800 --> 13:28.620
And these constraints channel chaotic chemical energy into organized metabolic work.

13:29.060 --> 13:39.960
But the critical difference is that the organism continuously recreates its own constraints using the very metabolic processes those constraints enable.

13:40.160 --> 13:40.380
Right.

13:40.380 --> 13:43.320
It's a continuous endogenous bootstrapping operation.

13:43.520 --> 13:43.920
Endogenous.

13:44.220 --> 13:44.480
Exactly.

13:44.480 --> 13:48.580
Like, the enzymes catalyze the construction of the lipid membrane.

13:48.900 --> 13:53.300
And then the membrane provides the internal environment that protects the enzymes.

13:53.680 --> 13:59.200
And together, they import nutrients to synthesize more enzymes and maintain the membrane.

13:59.620 --> 14:00.680
It's a perfect loop.

14:00.680 --> 14:01.560
Yeah.

14:01.560 --> 14:04.640
Every biological process depends on a constraint.

14:05.360 --> 14:10.300
And every vital constraint is continually produced by a process within the system.

14:11.080 --> 14:13.040
And that is constraint closure.

14:13.680 --> 14:20.180
And we really must factor in the thermodynamic reality here, which is where that entropy density

14:20.180 --> 14:22.880
field, S, comes into play.

14:23.000 --> 14:23.220
Right.

14:23.380 --> 14:25.320
The third letter in RSVP.

14:25.320 --> 14:25.800
Yes.

14:26.460 --> 14:33.500
Stuart Kaufman's work constraint cycle demonstrates that an organism exists in a universe that constantly

14:33.500 --> 14:35.640
pushes toward maximum entropy.

14:35.980 --> 14:37.540
The universe wants to mess things up.

14:37.880 --> 14:38.220
Always.

14:38.860 --> 14:43.100
The surrounding environment is constantly degrading the organism's constraints.

14:43.660 --> 14:49.320
The thermodynamic pressure is basically always trying to dissolve the cell back into a disorganized

14:49.320 --> 14:49.920
chemical suit.

14:49.920 --> 14:50.540
Exactly.

14:51.300 --> 14:57.340
So, to survive, the internal loop must utilize energy to locally invert that entropy gradient.

14:57.400 --> 14:57.700
Okay.

14:58.060 --> 15:02.640
It has to reconstruct its physical structures faster than the environment can tear them down.

15:02.640 --> 15:08.780
Which leads directly to the core mathematical definition in this whole framework, the endomorphism

15:08.780 --> 15:09.000
loop.

15:09.200 --> 15:09.640
Yes.

15:09.880 --> 15:16.140
And it is so vital to distinguish an endomorphism loop from just a standard physical cycle,

15:16.140 --> 15:24.840
like the water cycle, you know, evaporation, condensation, precipitation, that is a cycle, but it's not alive.

15:24.840 --> 15:25.240
Right.

15:25.560 --> 15:31.800
An endomorphism loop is a finite composition of transformations that actively restabilizes

15:31.800 --> 15:35.320
its own constraint structure under entropy flow.

15:35.620 --> 15:38.520
The paper calls this the CLIO condition.

15:38.740 --> 15:40.200
Yes, the CLIO condition.

15:40.200 --> 15:46.640
The loop doesn't just passively spin, it must maintain its constraints up to what they call

15:46.640 --> 15:48.120
admissible equivalence.

15:48.260 --> 15:50.020
It's an invariance condition.

15:50.160 --> 15:50.460
Right.

15:50.580 --> 15:56.500
The loop actively restabilizes itself within the viable region of the topological field

15:56.500 --> 15:59.560
space, which prevents its own thermodynamic collapse.

15:59.640 --> 16:05.380
Here's where it gets really interesting, because this mathematical formalization entirely resolves

16:05.380 --> 16:07.560
the heartbeat paradox we were talking about earlier.

16:07.660 --> 16:08.440
It does.

16:08.480 --> 16:09.520
It does it beautifully.

16:09.520 --> 16:10.000
Yeah.

16:10.420 --> 16:17.340
We map the human heart not in the flat phase space of standard physics, but in the RSVP field,

16:18.060 --> 16:19.940
searching for an endomorphism loop.

16:20.300 --> 16:25.400
So, does the act of pumping blood sit on the self-maintaining loop?

16:25.600 --> 16:25.980
Yes.

16:26.780 --> 16:30.400
Pumping blood delivers oxygen and nutrients to the cardiac tissue.

16:30.700 --> 16:35.760
The cardiac tissue metabolizes those resources to regenerate the heart muscle.

16:36.460 --> 16:38.620
The heart muscle continues to pump blood.

16:38.620 --> 16:39.620
It feeds itself.

16:39.620 --> 16:39.960
It feeds itself.

16:40.100 --> 16:40.460
Exactly.

16:40.660 --> 16:45.700
The process is functionally integrated into the constraint closure of the organism.

16:45.940 --> 16:47.780
But then what about the acoustic waves?

16:48.040 --> 16:48.340
Right.

16:48.720 --> 16:54.940
The sound waves just radiate out into the chest cavity, vibrate the sternum, and dissipate into

16:54.940 --> 16:55.720
the ambient air.

16:55.840 --> 16:59.500
They do not feed back into the endomorphism loop.

16:59.500 --> 17:03.900
They provide zero constraint regenerating utility to the cardiac tissue.

17:04.020 --> 17:05.720
They sit entirely off the loop.

17:05.840 --> 17:06.260
Exactly.

17:06.580 --> 17:13.060
Therefore, mathematically, pumping blood is an explicit function and generating sound is

17:13.060 --> 17:14.820
definitively epiphenomenal.

17:14.820 --> 17:18.160
The math derives the distinction autonomously.

17:18.160 --> 17:18.920
Which is crazy.

17:19.040 --> 17:22.940
We don't have to inject any subjective human interpretation of purpose.

17:23.120 --> 17:24.080
It's just math.

17:24.240 --> 17:27.880
And the implications here for defining agency are profound.

17:28.380 --> 17:36.020
I mean, proposition 2.4 demonstrates that function and agency are derivable topological

17:36.020 --> 17:36.680
facts.

17:36.860 --> 17:37.640
Give me an example.

17:37.640 --> 17:46.140
Consider bacterial chemotaxis, a single-celled bacterium swimming up a chemical gradient toward

17:46.140 --> 17:49.620
a nutrient source or away from a toxin.

17:49.680 --> 17:49.820
Right.

17:49.920 --> 17:53.080
We constantly anthropomorphize that behavior.

17:53.400 --> 17:53.660
Right.

17:53.760 --> 17:58.460
We say the bacterium wants the food or decides to swim away from the poison.

17:58.680 --> 17:58.980
Right.

17:59.020 --> 18:00.640
But it doesn't have a nervous system.

18:00.800 --> 18:03.860
It isn't engaging in cognitive deliberation.

18:04.060 --> 18:04.520
Exactly.

18:04.520 --> 18:11.760
But in the RSVP framework, agency requires no psychological attribution at all.

18:12.440 --> 18:17.920
It is mathematically defined as constraint-preserving trajectory modulation.

18:18.580 --> 18:21.560
Think of a tightrope walker crossing a gorge.

18:21.920 --> 18:22.220
Okay.

18:22.700 --> 18:25.280
A sudden gust of wind pushes them to the left.

18:25.700 --> 18:29.360
Instantly, they shift their weight, adjusting their balancing pole to the right.

18:29.560 --> 18:29.840
Yeah.

18:29.840 --> 18:34.660
They aren't stopping to philosophize about the aerodynamic properties of the wind.

18:34.740 --> 18:35.820
No, they just react.

18:35.980 --> 18:41.640
They are executing a continuous dynamic physical policy to avoid falling out of the viable

18:41.640 --> 18:43.980
state, which is, you know, staying on the rope.

18:44.120 --> 18:48.900
And the bacterium is executing the exact same physical policy in a chemical gradient.

18:49.020 --> 18:55.800
It modulates its trajectory purely to keep its internal endomorphism loop mathematically

18:55.800 --> 18:58.800
invariant against thermodynamic decay.

18:58.800 --> 19:01.320
It's truly a stunning synthesis.

19:01.840 --> 19:07.260
Agency is just stripped of vitalism entirely and reduced to topological persistence.

19:07.800 --> 19:07.960
Wow.

19:08.440 --> 19:15.560
But scaling this framework up from a single-celled bacterium to a complex multicellular organism

19:15.560 --> 19:19.300
requires an entirely new layer of geometry.

19:19.540 --> 19:23.000
Because a human being is not a single isolated endomorphism loop.

19:23.000 --> 19:28.820
We are an astronomically complex architecture composed of trillions of interacting.

19:28.820 --> 19:29.420
He's just hands.

