So in topology, a sheath is basically a tool for systematically tracking locally defined

19:50.100 --> 19:53.780
data attached to the open sets of a topological space.

19:53.880 --> 19:54.140
Okay.

19:54.420 --> 19:59.000
In this specific application, the base space is the hierarchy of biological scales.

19:59.360 --> 20:04.800
So you have the cellular scale, then the tissue scale, the organ scale, the organismic scale,

20:04.800 --> 20:06.960
and finally the ecological scale.

20:07.280 --> 20:09.160
It's a strictly nested hierarchy.

20:09.160 --> 20:09.640
Exactly.

20:10.180 --> 20:16.280
And for every distinct region at each of these scales, the sheath assigns what mathematicians

20:16.280 --> 20:17.540
call a stalk.

20:17.900 --> 20:18.540
A stalk.

20:18.660 --> 20:19.480
Like on a plant.

20:19.800 --> 20:20.120
Yeah.

20:20.340 --> 20:21.380
Mathematically speaking.

20:21.780 --> 20:28.440
The stalk represents the space of all possible admissible closure configurations at that specific

20:28.440 --> 20:29.340
local level.

20:29.480 --> 20:29.720
Okay.

20:29.860 --> 20:33.800
So the cellular stalk contains the local metabolic loops.

20:34.020 --> 20:37.960
The organ stalk contains the higher level functional loops.

20:38.100 --> 20:38.360
Right.

20:38.360 --> 20:44.120
The critical mechanism of the sheath is how these disparate scales interact without annihilating

20:44.120 --> 20:44.700
